    "h": 824,
    "thumb": "images/observations/observations/cover-t.webp"
  },
  "text": "Aliscioni's photographic work is rooted in the direct observation of reality and develops through an ongoing exploration centred primarily on the human figure, its relationship with the surrounding environment, and the conditions in which everyday life unfolds.\n\nTaken across different places and contexts, the images do not follow a predetermined narrative structure, nor do they seek to provide a descriptive account of the territories encountered. People, architecture, landscapes and everyday situations are observed without any particular hierarchy, as elements of the same reality.\n\nThe focus lies on human presence, behaviour, relationships and the material conditions of existence. The subjects are neither idealised nor turned into characters. They remain part of a concrete, often complex context, in which spaces and objects also contribute to the construction of the image. Photography thus takes on a predominantly observational approach. Rather than seeking extraordinary events, it focuses on ordinary situations, suspended moments and details that would normally escape a hurried glance.\n\nThe result is a collection of independent images, free from narrative conclusions, in which the relationship between the individual, the environment and reality becomes the central element of continuity.\n\nAn open-ended body of work that does not claim to interpret or explain what it observes, but instead presents a personal and unfiltered view of the world.",
